    "question": {
      ":question/id": "34b21e68-baa7-58f0-a7d6-41e1b74e01bf",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/baseline-samples/17592186096700/assets/b883b51d22a321b0e147c27e5a7b9720a0216a39a6ea3dca7d993e09c5eb8acc.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $p=3r$\n\nII. $q=2s$\n\nIII. $t=-q$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $p$ and $r$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $p$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $r$. So, $p\\ne 3r$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $q$ and $s$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $q$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $s$. So, $q=2s$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $t$ and $q$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $t$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $q$. So, $t\\ne -q$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
